    "['cfm version [version]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ethernet cfm global', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'ethernet cfm global', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nethernet cfm global\\nethernet cfm domain standard level 7\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Huawei `cfm version standard` command enables the standard version of Connectivity Fault Management (CFM) globally. \\n- In Cisco, the equivalent is configuring CFM globally (`ethernet cfm global`) and setting the CFM domain standard with a level (I used level 7 as it's commonly used for operator-level domains, but the exact level would depend on your network hierarchy).\\n- The Cisco implementation is more explicit, requiring both global enablement and domain definition.\\n\\nNote: The exact level number in Cisco should match your network's CFM level design, as CFM uses a hierarchical level system (0-7).\", 'trans_template': 'ethernet cfm global'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ethernet cfm domain standard level 7', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'ethernet cfm domain standard level 7', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nethernet cfm global\\nethernet cfm domain standard level 7\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Huawei `cfm version standard` command enables the standard version of Connectivity Fault Management (CFM) globally. \\n- In Cisco, the equivalent is configuring CFM globally (`ethernet cfm global`) and setting the CFM domain standard with a level (I used level 7 as it's commonly used for operator-level domains, but the exact level would depend on your network hierarchy).\\n- The Cisco implementation is more explicit, requiring both global enablement and domain definition.\\n\\nNote: The exact level number in Cisco should match your network's CFM level design, as CFM uses a hierarchical level system (0-7).\", 'trans_template': 'ethernet cfm domain standard level 7'}]]": 1,
