    "['l2 binding vsi [vsi-name]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'l2vpn xconnect context ldp1', 'parent_command': ['interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1.1'], 'root': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nl2vpn xconnect context ldp1\\n## \\n\\nAlternatively, if using an EVPN-based approach (modern Cisco implementations), it could also be:\\n##\\nxconnect 2.2.2.2 1 encapsulation mpls\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact Cisco equivalent depends on the platform and MPLS/VPLS implementation method. The first translation assumes a traditional L2VPN VPLS configuration, while the second shows an explicit MPLS pseudowire establishment. The VSI-ID \"1\" from the Huawei configuration becomes the VC-ID in Cisco (shown as \"1\" in the second example).', 'trans_template': 'l2vpn xconnect context ldp1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'xconnect 2.2.2.2 1 encapsulation mpls', 'parent_command': ['interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1.1'], 'root': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nl2vpn xconnect context ldp1\\n## \\n\\nAlternatively, if using an EVPN-based approach (modern Cisco implementations), it could also be:\\n##\\nxconnect 2.2.2.2 1 encapsulation mpls\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact Cisco equivalent depends on the platform and MPLS/VPLS implementation method. The first translation assumes a traditional L2VPN VPLS configuration, while the second shows an explicit MPLS pseudowire establishment. The VSI-ID \"1\" from the Huawei configuration becomes the VC-ID in Cisco (shown as \"1\" in the second example).', 'trans_template': 'xconnect 2.2.2.2 1 encapsulation mpls'}]]": 1,
    "['interface [interface-name]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/2', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/2', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/2\\nno shutdown\\n##\\n\\nNote: The translation is simplified since Cisco doesn't use the exact same interface naming convention (Huawei's `GigabitEthernet1/0/2` would typically be `GigabitEthernet0/1` or similar in Cisco). The core functionality (`no shutdown`) is preserved, but the exact interface identifier would need to be adjusted based on the Cisco device's physical layout.\", 'trans_template': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/2'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no shutdown', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/2\\nno shutdown\\n##\\n\\nNote: The translation is simplified since Cisco doesn't use the exact same interface naming convention (Huawei's `GigabitEthernet1/0/2` would typically be `GigabitEthernet0/1` or similar in Cisco). The core functionality (`no shutdown`) is preserved, but the exact interface identifier would need to be adjusted based on the Cisco device's physical layout.\", 'trans_template': 'no shutdown'}]]": 1,
