    "['area [area-id]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'area 0', 'parent_command': ['router ospf 1'], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\narea 0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:  \\nIn Cisco IOS, OSPF area numbers can be written in standard decimal format (e.g., `area 0`) rather than the dotted-decimal notation (`0.0.0.0`) used by Huawei. Both represent the OSPF backbone area. The Cisco equivalent is simpler while maintaining the same functionality.', 'trans_template': 'area 0'}]]": 1,
    "['network [ip-address] [wildcard-mask]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0', 'parent_command': ['router ospf 1', 'area 0'], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nnetwork 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nIn Cisco OSPF configuration:\\n1. The `network` command syntax remains similar\\n2. The Huawei wildcard mask `0.0.0.0` (exact match) translates directly to Cisco\\n3. The area must be explicitly stated in Cisco (Huawei's area was configured in the previous line)\\n4. Area 0 (backbone area) matches the Huawei `area 0.0.0.0` context\", 'trans_template': 'network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0'}]]": 1,
    "['network [ip-address] [wildcard-mask]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'parent_command': ['router ospf 1', 'area 0'], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nnetwork 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\n- In Cisco OSPF configuration, the `network` command requires specifying the area directly (unlike Huawei where area is configured under a separate hierarchy).\\n- The wildcard mask (0.0.0.255) remains the same between Huawei and Cisco.\\n- The area ID (0) is appended in Cisco syntax, matching Huawei's area 0.0.0.0 (which is equivalent to area 0 in Cisco).\", 'trans_template': 'network 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0'}]]": 1,
    "['cfm md [md-name]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ethernet cfm domain md1 level 7', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'ethernet cfm domain md1 level 7', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nethernet cfm domain md1 level 7\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Cisco IOS, the equivalent command to create a Connectivity Fault Management (CFM) maintenance domain is `ethernet cfm domain`. \\n- `md1` is the domain name, which is preserved in the translation.\\n- The `level 7` is added because CFM domains in Cisco require a maintenance level (0-7), with 7 being the highest. Huawei's standard version doesn't explicitly show the level in the configuration, but level 7 is a safe default for provider backbone bridging scenarios.\\n\\nNote: The exact level might need adjustment based on your network hierarchy, as Huawei's standard version may imply a different default level.\", 'trans_template': 'ethernet cfm domain md1 level 7'}]]": 1,
    "['ma [ma-name]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ethernet cfm domain md1 service ma1', 'parent_command': ['ethernet cfm domain md1 level 7'], 'root': 'ethernet cfm domain md1 level 7', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nethernet cfm domain md1 service ma1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Huawei `ma ma1` command under CFM (Connectivity Fault Management) configuration creates a Maintenance Association (MA). In Cisco's equivalent CFM configuration, this translates to `ethernet cfm domain [domain-name] service [service-name]`, where:\\n- `md1` is the Maintenance Domain (MD)\\n- `ma1` is the Maintenance Association (MA)\\n\\nThe Cisco command establishes a CFM service instance within a specified domain, which matches Huawei's MA creation under an MD context.\", 'trans_template': 'ethernet cfm domain md1 service ma1'}]]": 1,
    "['map vsi [vsi-name]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'xconnect 2.2.2.2 1 encapsulation mpls', 'parent_command': ['ethernet cfm domain md1 level 7', 'ethernet cfm domain md1 service ma1'], 'root': 'ethernet cfm domain md1 level 7', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'For the Huawei command `map vsi ldp1`, the equivalent Cisco command (for L2VPN VPLS or VPWS configurations) would be:\\n\\n##\\nxconnect 2.2.2.2 1 encapsulation mpls\\n##\\n\\n**Explanation**:\\n- In Cisco, `xconnect` binds the attachment circuit (like a subinterface) to a pseudowire with a remote PE (2.2.2.2, the peer LSR-ID from the Huawei config) and a VC ID (1, matching the `vsi-id` in the Huawei config).\\n- The Huawei `map vsi ldp1` associates the CFM MA (Maintenance Association) with the VSI instance, while Cisco typically ties connectivity directly to the pseudowire under the service instance.\\n\\nNote: The exact Cisco equivalent may vary slightly depending on the deployment context (VPLS or VPWS). The above assumes a basic MPLS L2VPN pseudowire scenario.', 'trans_template': 'xconnect 2.2.2.2 1 encapsulation mpls'}]]": 1,
    "['mep mep-id [id] interface [interface-name] [direction]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ethernet cfm mep domain md1 service ma1 mep 2 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1.1 direction up', 'parent_command': ['ethernet cfm domain md1 level 7', 'ethernet cfm domain md1 service ma1'], 'root': 'ethernet cfm domain md1 level 7', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the HUAWEI `mep mep-id 2 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1.1 inward` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nethernet cfm mep domain md1 service ma1 mep 2 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1.1 direction up\\n##\\n\\nThis Cisco command:\\n1. Creates a MEP (Maintenance End Point) with ID 2\\n2. Associates it with interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1.1\\n3. Sets the direction to \"up\" (equivalent to HUAWEI\\'s \"inward\")\\n4. Places it within the specified domain (md1) and service (ma1) context\\n\\nThe \"inward\" direction in HUAWEI translates to \"up\" in Cisco terminology, indicating the MEP faces toward the bridge.', 'trans_template': 'ethernet cfm mep domain md1 service ma1 mep 2 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1.1 direction up'}]]": 1,
    "['mep ccm-send mep-id [id] enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ethernet cfm mep 2 service instance 1 continuity-check transmission', 'parent_command': ['ethernet cfm domain md1 level 7', 'ethernet cfm domain md1 service ma1'], 'root': 'ethernet cfm domain md1 level 7', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for `mep ccm-send mep-id 2 enable`:\\n\\n##\\nethernet cfm mep 2 service instance 1 continuity-check transmission\\n##\\n\\nThis Cisco command enables CCM (Continuity Check Message) transmission for MEP (Maintenance End Point) with ID 2, which is functionally equivalent to the Huawei command. The service instance number (1 in this example) would need to match your Cisco service instance configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'ethernet cfm mep 2 service instance 1 continuity-check transmission'}]]": 1,
